Begin Exploring Related Columns
Below is a compact “road‑map” for building the requested RFM‑based analysis together with ten exploratory / progressively more complex SQLite queries.  
The idea is to let you inspect interim data at every step before finally producing the average sales per order in each RFM segment.

────────────────────────────────────────
1. Relevant columns and their potential usage
• orders.order_id : primary key to link orders with items & payments.  
• orders.order_status : filter to keep only 'delivered' orders.  
• orders.order_purchase_timestamp : purchase date – used to calculate Recency.  
• customers.customer_id → customers.customer_unique_id : maps each order to the “real” customer.  
• order_items.order_id, order_items.price, order_items.freight_value : monetary value of every item.  
• (optional) order_items.seller_id / products.* : irrelevant to RFM itself, so ignored here.

Conditions that will be used
• orders.order_status = 'delivered'  
• dates are kept in text ISO format, therefore can be compared lexicographically.  
• Recency will be measured in days:  
  recency = julianday(MAX(order_purchase_timestamp_overall)) − julianday(customer_last_purchase)

RFM segmentation criteria (one possible, quartile‑based example)
• Build three scores (1‑4) for Recency (R), Frequency (F) and Monetary (M) by cutting the distribution of each metric into quartiles.  
  – R = 1 means “most recent purchases”, R = 4 means “old purchases”.  
  – F = 4 means “many orders”, F = 1 means “few orders”.  
  – M = 4 means “high monetary value”, M = 1 means “low monetary value”.  
• Concatenate the three scores into a label such as ‘R1F4M3’.  
• Groups can subsequently be mapped to business‑friendly names (Champions, Loyal, At‑Risk, etc.).

Average sales per order for a customer
avg_sales = (Σ price + Σ freight_value) / number_of_orders

────────────────────────────────────────
2. Ten exploratory SQL queries
(Every query is limited to 20 rows; feel free to remove the LIMIT clause once you are confident.)

```sql
-- 1. Distinct order statuses – check that 'delivered' exists
SELECT DISTINCT order_status
FROM orders
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Look at 20 delivered orders and their purchase time‑stamps
SELECT order_id,
       order_purchase_timestamp
FROM orders
WHERE order_status = 'delivered'
ORDER BY order_purchase_timestamp DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Join orders → customers to discover the customer_unique_id
SELECT o.order_id,
       c.customer_unique_id
FROM orders AS o
JOIN customers AS c
  ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id
WHERE o.order_status = 'delivered'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Inspect monetary values inside order_items
SELECT order_id,
       order_item_id,
       price,
       freight_value
FROM order_items
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Compute total value per order (price + freight) – sample 20 rows
SELECT oi.order_id,
       ROUND(SUM(oi.price + oi.freight_value), 2) AS order_total
FROM order_items AS oi
GROUP BY oi.order_id
ORDER BY order_total DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Combine delivered orders with their totals
SELECT o.order_id,
       c.customer_unique_id,
       o.order_purchase_timestamp,
       ROUND(SUM(oi.price + oi.freight_value), 2) AS order_total
FROM orders AS o
JOIN customers AS c
  ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id
JOIN order_items AS oi
  ON o.order_id = oi.order_id
WHERE o.order_status = 'delivered'
GROUP BY o.order_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Get per‑customer aggregates: number of orders, total spend, last purchase
WITH delivered AS (
  SELECT o.order_id,
         c.customer_unique_id,
         o.order_purchase_timestamp,
         SUM(oi.price + oi.freight_value) AS order_total
  FROM orders AS o
  JOIN customers AS c
    ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id
  JOIN order_items AS oi
    ON o.order_id = oi.order_id
  WHERE o.order_status = 'delivered'
  GROUP BY o.order_id
)
SELECT customer_unique_id,
       COUNT(*)                        AS orders_cnt,
       ROUND(SUM(order_total), 2)      AS total_spend,
       MAX(order_purchase_timestamp)   AS last_purchase
FROM delivered
GROUP BY customer_unique_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Compute Recency in days for each customer
WITH max_date AS (
  SELECT MAX(order_purchase_timestamp) AS max_ts
  FROM orders
  WHERE order_status = 'delivered'
),
customer_metrics AS (
  SELECT c.customer_unique_id,
         COUNT(o.order_id)                         AS orders_cnt,
         ROUND(SUM(oi.price + oi.freight_value),2) AS total_spend,
         MAX(o.order_purchase_timestamp)           AS last_purchase
  FROM orders AS o
  JOIN customers AS c
    ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id
  JOIN order_items AS oi
    ON o.order_id = oi.order_id
  WHERE o.order_status = 'delivered'
  GROUP BY c.customer_unique_id
)
SELECT cm.*,
       ROUND(julianday(md.max_ts) - julianday(cm.last_purchase), 1) AS recency_days
FROM customer_metrics AS cm, max_date AS md
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Assign quartile scores R, F, M to every customer
WITH max_date AS (
  SELECT MAX(order_purchase_timestamp) AS max_ts
  FROM orders
  WHERE order_status = 'delivered'
),
base AS (
  SELECT c.customer_unique_id,
         COUNT(o.order_id)                         AS orders_cnt,
         ROUND(SUM(oi.price + oi.freight_value),2) AS total_spend,
         MAX(o.order_purchase_timestamp)           AS last_purchase
  FROM orders AS o
  JOIN customers AS c
    ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id
  JOIN order_items AS oi
    ON o.order_id = oi.order_id
  WHERE o.order_status = 'delivered'
  GROUP BY c.customer_unique_id
),
with_recency AS (
  SELECT *,
         ROUND(julianday((SELECT max_ts FROM max_date)) - julianday(last_purchase),1) AS recency_days
  FROM base
),
quantiles AS (
  SELECT
    percentile_25(recency_days)  AS r25,
    percentile_50(recency_days)  AS r50,
    percentile_75(recency_days)  AS r75,
    percentile_25(orders_cnt)    AS f25,
    percentile_50(orders_cnt)    AS f50,
    percentile_75(orders_cnt)    AS f75,
    percentile_25(total_spend)   AS m25,
    percentile_50(total_spend)   AS m50,
    percentile_75(total_spend)   AS m75
  FROM with_recency
)
SELECT wr.customer_unique_id,
       wr.recency_days,
       wr.orders_cnt,
       wr.total_spend,
       -- Recency score (1 = freshest)
       CASE
         WHEN wr.recency_days <= q.r25 THEN 1
         WHEN wr.recency_days <= q.r50 THEN 2
         WHEN wr.recency_days <= q.r75 THEN 3
         ELSE 4
       END AS R,
       -- Frequency score (4 = most orders)
       CASE
         WHEN wr.orders_cnt <= q.f25 THEN 1
         WHEN wr.orders_cnt <= q.f50 THEN 2
         WHEN wr.orders_cnt <= q.f75 THEN 3
         ELSE 4
       END AS F,
       -- Monetary score (4 = highest spend)
       CASE
         WHEN wr.total_spend <= q.m25 THEN 1
         WHEN wr.total_spend <= q.m50 THEN 2
         WHEN wr.total_spend <= q.m75 THEN 3
         ELSE 4
       END AS M
FROM with_recency AS wr
CROSS JOIN quantiles AS q
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL: Average sales per order for each customer, then aggregated by RFM segment
WITH max_date AS (
  SELECT MAX(order_purchase_timestamp) AS max_ts
  FROM orders
  WHERE order_status = 'delivered'
),
base AS (
  SELECT c.customer_unique_id,
         COUNT(o.order_id)                         AS orders_cnt,
         ROUND(SUM(oi.price + oi.freight_value),2) AS total_spend,
         MAX(o.order_purchase_timestamp)           AS last_purchase
  FROM orders AS o
  JOIN customers AS c
    ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id
  JOIN order_items AS oi
    ON o.order_id = oi.order_id
  WHERE o.order_status = 'delivered'
  GROUP BY c.customer_unique_id
),
with_recency AS (
  SELECT *,
         ROUND(julianday((SELECT max_ts FROM max_date)) - julianday(last_purchase),1) AS recency_days
  FROM base
),
quantiles AS (
  SELECT
    percentile_25(recency_days)  AS r25,
    percentile_50(recency_days)  AS r50,
    percentile_75(recency_days)  AS r75,
    percentile_25(orders_cnt)    AS f25,
    percentile_50(orders_cnt)    AS f50,
    percentile_75(orders_cnt)    AS f75,
    percentile_25(total_spend)   AS m25,
    percentile_50(total_spend)   AS m50,
    percentile_75(total_spend)   AS m75
  FROM with_recency
),
scored AS (
  SELECT wr.*,
         -- R, F, M scores
         CASE
           WHEN wr.recency_days <= q.r25 THEN 1
           WHEN wr.recency_days <= q.r50 THEN 2
           WHEN wr.recency_days <= q.r75 THEN 3
           ELSE 4
         END AS R,
         CASE
           WHEN wr.orders_cnt <= q.f25 THEN 1
           WHEN wr.orders_cnt <= q.f50 THEN 2
           WHEN wr.orders_cnt <= q.f75 THEN 3
           ELSE 4
         END AS F,
         CASE
           WHEN wr.total_spend <= q.m25 THEN 1
           WHEN wr.total_spend <= q.m50 THEN 2
           WHEN wr.total_spend <= q.m75 THEN 3
           ELSE 4
         END AS M
  FROM with_recency AS wr
  CROSS JOIN quantiles AS q
),
rfm_segment AS (
  SELECT customer_unique_id,
         orders_cnt,
         total_spend,
         ROUND(total_spend*1.0/orders_cnt,2) AS avg_sales_per_order,
         recency_days,
         R||F||M AS segment_code
  FROM scored
)
SELECT segment_code,
       COUNT(*)                              AS customers_in_segment,
       ROUND(AVG(avg_sales_per_order),2)     AS avg_sales_per_order_segment
FROM rfm_segment
GROUP BY segment_code
ORDER BY segment_code
LIMIT 20;
```
(Replace percentile_xx() with your own quartile helper if not available in your SQLite build; you can use window functions or a pre‑computed table of cut‑offs.)

